#86001
"Shallows of a River, South Bank"
"The water from the river swirls slowly here, moving far slower than the water
in the middle of the stream.  The crumbled rock from the once-standing bridge
supports block most of the river's movement, causing stagnant pools of dead
weeds and fallen leaves to form here.  To the south is a ten-foot bank of mud
that has slid partially into the water, making the climb out of the river
relatively easy.  Moving north would bring you toward the middle of the river,
the visible riverbed suggesting the water isn't too deep there.  To the west
is more shallows, although the overhang of the bank in that direction hangs
ominously over the water like a giant, muddy wave about to break.  Travel east
is blocked by chunks of rock and beams of wood.
"
"slowly make your way up the bank (south).
"
